#. type: Title =
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "git-commit(1)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:5
#, no-wrap
msgid "NAME"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:7
msgid "git-commit - Record changes to the repository"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:9
#, no-wrap
msgid "SYNOPSIS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"git commit [-a | --interactive | --patch] [-s] [-v] [-u[<mode>]] [--amend]\n"
"\t   [--dry-run] [(-c | -C | --squash) <commit> | --fixup "
"[(amend|reword):]<commit>]\n"
"\t   [-F <file> | -m <msg>] [--reset-author] [--allow-empty]\n"
"\t   [--allow-empty-message] [--no-verify] [-e] [--author=<author>]\n"
"\t   [--date=<date>] [--cleanup=<mode>] [--[no-]status]\n"
"\t   [-i | -o] [--pathspec-from-file=<file> [--pathspec-file-nul]]\n"
"\t   [(--trailer <token>[(=|:)<value>])...] [-S[<keyid>]]\n"
"\t   [--] [<pathspec>...]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:21
#, no-wrap
msgid "DESCRIPTION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:28
msgid ""
"Create a new commit containing the current contents of the index and the "
"given log message describing the changes. The new commit is a direct child "
"of HEAD, usually the tip of the current branch, and the branch is updated to "
"point to it (unless no branch is associated with the working tree, in which "
"case `HEAD` is \"detached\" as described in linkgit:git-checkout[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:30
msgid "The content to be committed can be specified in several ways:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:34
msgid ""
"by using linkgit:git-add[1] to incrementally \"add\" changes to the index "
"before using the `commit` command (Note: even modified files must be "
"\"added\");"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:37
msgid ""
"by using linkgit:git-rm[1] to remove files from the working tree and the "
"index, again before using the `commit` command;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:43
msgid ""
"by listing files as arguments to the `commit` command (without "
"`--interactive` or `--patch` switch), in which case the commit will ignore "
"changes staged in the index, and instead record the current content of the "
"listed files (which must already be known to Git);"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:49
msgid ""
"by using the `-a` switch with the `commit` command to automatically \"add\" "
"changes from all known files (i.e. all files that are already listed in the "
"index) and to automatically \"rm\" files in the index that have been removed "
"from the working tree, and then perform the actual commit;"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:55
msgid ""
"by using the `--interactive` or `--patch` switches with the `commit` command "
"to decide one by one which files or hunks should be part of the commit in "
"addition to contents in the index, before finalizing the operation. See the "
"``Interactive Mode'' section of linkgit:git-add[1] to learn how to operate "
"these modes."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:59
msgid ""
"The `--dry-run` option can be used to obtain a summary of what is included "
"by any of the above for the next commit by giving the same set of parameters "
"(options and paths)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:62
msgid ""
"If you make a commit and then find a mistake immediately after that, you can "
"recover from it with `git reset`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "OPTIONS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:67
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-a`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--all`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:72
msgid ""
"Automatically stage files that have been modified and deleted, but new files "
"you have not told Git about are not affected."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:73
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-p`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:74
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--patch`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:78
msgid ""
"Use the interactive patch selection interface to choose which changes to "
"commit. See linkgit:git-add[1] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:81
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-C <commit>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reuse-message=<commit>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:86
msgid ""
"Take an existing _<commit>_ object, and reuse the log message and the "
"authorship information (including the timestamp)  when creating the commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-c <commit>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:88
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reedit-message=<commit>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:91
msgid ""
"Like `-C`, but with `-c` the editor is invoked, so that the user can further "
"edit the commit message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--fixup=[(amend|reword):]<commit>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:102
msgid ""
"Create a new commit which \"fixes up\" _<commit>_ when applied with `git "
"rebase --autosquash`. Plain `--fixup=<commit>` creates a \"fixup!\" commit "
"which changes the content of _<commit>_ but leaves its log message "
"untouched. `--fixup=amend:<commit>` is similar but creates an \"amend!\" "
"commit which also replaces the log message of _<commit>_ with the log "
"message of the \"amend!\" commit.  `--fixup=reword:<commit>` creates an "
"\"amend!\" commit which replaces the log message of _<commit>_ with its own "
"log message but makes no changes to the content of _<commit>_."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:110
msgid ""
"The commit created by plain `--fixup=<commit>` has a title composed of "
"\"fixup!\" followed by the title of _<commit>_, and is recognized specially "
"by `git rebase --autosquash`. The `-m` option may be used to supplement the "
"log message of the created commit, but the additional commentary will be "
"thrown away once the \"fixup!\" commit is squashed into _<commit>_ by `git "
"rebase --autosquash`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:120
msgid ""
"The commit created by `--fixup=amend:<commit>` is similar but its title is "
"instead prefixed with \"amend!\". The log message of _<commit>_ is copied "
"into the log message of the \"amend!\" commit and opened in an editor so it "
"can be refined. When `git rebase --autosquash` squashes the \"amend!\" "
"commit into _<commit>_, the log message of _<commit>_ is replaced by the "
"refined log message from the \"amend!\" commit. It is an error for the "
"\"amend!\" commit's log message to be empty unless `--allow-empty-message` "
"is specified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:126
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`--fixup=reword:<commit>` is shorthand for `--fixup=amend:<commit>\n"
" --only`. It creates an \"amend!\" commit with only a log message\n"
"(ignoring any changes staged in the index). When squashed by `git\n"
"rebase --autosquash`, it replaces the log message of _<commit>_\n"
"without making any other changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:130
msgid ""
"Neither \"fixup!\" nor \"amend!\" commits change authorship of _<commit>_ "
"when applied by `git rebase --autosquash`.  See linkgit:git-rebase[1] for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:131
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--squash=<commit>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:137
msgid ""
"Construct a commit message for use with `git rebase --autosquash`.  The "
"commit message title is taken from the specified commit with a prefix of "
"\"squash! \".  Can be used with additional commit message options "
"(`-m`/`-c`/`-C`/`-F`). See linkgit:git-rebase[1] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:138
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--reset-author`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:143
msgid ""
"When used with `-C`/`-c`/`--amend` options, or when committing after a "
"conflicting cherry-pick, declare that the authorship of the resulting commit "
"now belongs to the committer. This also renews the author timestamp."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:144
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--short`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:147
msgid ""
"When doing a dry-run, give the output in the short-format. See "
"linkgit:git-status[1] for details. Implies `--dry-run`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:148
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--branch`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:150
msgid "Show the branch and tracking info even in short-format."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:151
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--porcelain`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:155
msgid ""
"When doing a dry-run, give the output in a porcelain-ready format. See "
"linkgit:git-status[1] for details. Implies `--dry-run`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--long`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:159
msgid ""
"When doing a dry-run, give the output in the long-format.  Implies "
"`--dry-run`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:160
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-z`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:161
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--null`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:168
msgid ""
"When showing `short` or `porcelain` status output, print the filename "
"verbatim and terminate the entries with _NUL_, instead of _LF_.  If no "
"format is given, implies the `--porcelain` output format.  Without the `-z` "
"option, filenames with \"unusual\" characters are quoted as explained for "
"the configuration variable `core.quotePath` (see linkgit:git-config[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:169
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-F <file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--file=<file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:173
msgid ""
"Take the commit message from _<file>_.  Use '-' to read the message from the "
"standard input."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:174
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--author=<author>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:180
msgid ""
"Override the commit author. Specify an explicit author using the standard `A "
"U Thor <author@example.com>` format. Otherwise _<author>_ is assumed to be a "
"pattern and is used to search for an existing commit by that author "
"(i.e. `git rev-list --all -i --author=<author>`); the commit author is then "
"copied from the first such commit found."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:181
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--date=<date>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:183
msgid "Override the author date used in the commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:184
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-m <msg>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:185
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--message=<msg>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:189
msgid ""
"Use _<msg>_ as the commit message.  If multiple `-m` options are given, "
"their values are concatenated as separate paragraphs."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:191
msgid "The `-m` option is mutually exclusive with `-c`, `-C`, and `-F`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:192
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-t <file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:193
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--template=<file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:202
msgid ""
"When editing the commit message, start the editor with the contents in "
"_<file>_.  The `commit.template` configuration variable is often used to "
"give this option implicitly to the command.  This mechanism can be used by "
"projects that want to guide participants with some hints on what to write in "
"the message in what order.  If the user exits the editor without editing the "
"message, the commit is aborted.  This has no effect when a message is given "
"by other means, e.g. with the `-m` or `-F` options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:205
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--trailer <token>[(=|:)<value>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:215
msgid ""
"Specify a (_<token>_, _<value>_) pair that should be applied as a "
"trailer. (e.g. `git commit --trailer \"Signed-off-by:C O Mitter \\ "
"<committer@example.com>\" --trailer \"Helped-by:C O Mitter \\ "
"<committer@example.com>\"` will add the `Signed-off-by` trailer and the "
"`Helped-by` trailer to the commit message.)  The `trailer.*` configuration "
"variables (linkgit:git-interpret-trailers[1]) can be used to define if a "
"duplicated trailer is omitted, where in the run of trailers each trailer "
"would appear, and other details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:216
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-n`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:217
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verify`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:218
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-verify`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:221
msgid ""
"Bypass the `pre-commit` and `commit-msg` hooks.  See also "
"linkgit:githooks[5]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:222
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--allow-empty`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:227
msgid ""
"Usually recording a commit that has the exact same tree as its sole parent "
"commit is a mistake, and the command prevents you from making such a "
"commit.  This option bypasses the safety, and is primarily for use by "
"foreign SCM interface scripts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:228
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--allow-empty-message`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:232
msgid ""
"Create a commit with an empty commit message without using plumbing commands "
"like linkgit:git-commit-tree[1]. Like `--allow-empty`, this command is "
"primarily for use by foreign SCM interface scripts."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:233
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--cleanup=<mode>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:237
msgid ""
"Determine how the supplied commit message should be cleaned up before "
"committing.  The '<mode>' can be `strip`, `whitespace`, `verbatim`, "
"`scissors` or `default`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:239
#, no-wrap
msgid "`strip`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:242
msgid ""
"Strip leading and trailing empty lines, trailing whitespace, commentary and "
"collapse consecutive empty lines."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:242
#, no-wrap
msgid "`whitespace`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:244
msgid "Same as `strip` except #commentary is not removed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:244
#, no-wrap
msgid "`verbatim`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:246
msgid "Do not change the message at all."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:246
#, no-wrap
msgid "`scissors`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:250
msgid ""
"Same as `whitespace` except that everything from (and including)  the line "
"found below is truncated, if the message is to be edited.  \"`#`\" can be "
"customized with `core.commentChar`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:252
#, no-wrap
msgid "# ------------------------ >8 ------------------------\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:253
#, no-wrap
msgid "`default`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:256
msgid "Same as `strip` if the message is to be edited.  Otherwise `whitespace`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:260
msgid ""
"The default can be changed by the `commit.cleanup` configuration variable "
"(see linkgit:git-config[1])."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:261
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-e`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:262
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--edit`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:266
msgid ""
"Let the user further edit the message taken from _<file>_ with `-F <file>`, "
"command line with `-m <message>`, and from _<commit>_ with `-C <commit>`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:267
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-edit`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:271
msgid ""
"Use the selected commit message without launching an editor.  For example, "
"`git commit --amend --no-edit` amends a commit without changing its commit "
"message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:272
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--amend`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:282
msgid ""
"Replace the tip of the current branch by creating a new commit. The recorded "
"tree is prepared as usual (including the effect of the `-i` and `-o` options "
"and explicit pathspec), and the message from the original commit is used as "
"the starting point, instead of an empty message, when no other message is "
"specified from the command line via options such as `-m`, `-F`, `-c`, etc.  "
"The new commit has the same parents and author as the current one (the "
"`--reset-author` option can countermand this)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:285
msgid "It is a rough equivalent for:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:290
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\t$ git reset --soft HEAD^\n"
"\t$ ... do something else to come up with the right tree ...\n"
"\t$ git commit -c ORIG_HEAD\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:293
msgid "but can be used to amend a merge commit."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:298
msgid ""
"You should understand the implications of rewriting history if you amend a "
"commit that has already been published.  (See the \"RECOVERING FROM UPSTREAM "
"REBASE\" section in linkgit:git-rebase[1].)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:299
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-post-rewrite`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:301
msgid "Bypass the `post-rewrite` hook."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:302
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-i`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:303
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--include`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:308
msgid ""
"Before making a commit out of staged contents so far, stage the contents of "
"paths given on the command line as well.  This is usually not what you want "
"unless you are concluding a conflicted merge."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:309
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-o`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:310
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--only`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:322
msgid ""
"Make a commit by taking the updated working tree contents of the paths "
"specified on the command line, disregarding any contents that have been "
"staged for other paths. This is the default mode of operation of `git "
"commit` if any paths are given on the command line, in which case this "
"option can be omitted.  If this option is specified together with `--amend`, "
"then no paths need to be specified, which can be used to amend the last "
"commit without committing changes that have already been staged. If used "
"together with `--allow-empty` paths are also not required, and an empty "
"commit will be created."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:323
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pathspec-from-file=<file>`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:330
msgid ""
"Pass pathspec in _<file>_ instead of commandline args. If _<file>_ is "
"exactly `-` then standard input is used. Pathspec elements are separated by "
"_LF_ or _CR_/_LF_. Pathspec elements can be quoted as explained for the "
"configuration variable `core.quotePath` (see linkgit:git-config[1]). See "
"also `--pathspec-file-nul` and global `--literal-pathspecs`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:331
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--pathspec-file-nul`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:335
msgid ""
"Only meaningful with `--pathspec-from-file`. Pathspec elements are separated "
"with _NUL_ character and all other characters are taken literally (including "
"newlines and quotes)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:336
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-u[<mode>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:337
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--untracked-files[=<mode>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:339
msgid "Show untracked files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:344
msgid ""
"The _<mode>_ parameter is optional (defaults to `all`), and is used to "
"specify the handling of untracked files; when `-u` is not used, the default "
"is `normal`, i.e. show untracked files and directories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:346
msgid "The possible options are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:347
#, no-wrap
msgid "`no`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:348
msgid "Show no untracked files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:348
#, no-wrap
msgid "`normal`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:349
msgid "Shows untracked files and directories"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:349
#, no-wrap
msgid "`all`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:350
msgid "Also shows individual files in untracked directories."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:355
msgid ""
"All usual spellings for Boolean value `true` are taken as `normal` and "
"`false` as `no`.  The default can be changed using the "
"`status.showUntrackedFiles` configuration variable documented in "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:357
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-v`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:358
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--verbose`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:367
msgid ""
"Show unified diff between the `HEAD` commit and what would be committed at "
"the bottom of the commit message template to help the user describe the "
"commit by reminding what changes the commit has.  Note that this diff output "
"doesn't have its lines prefixed with `#`. This diff will not be a part of "
"the commit message. See the `commit.verbose` configuration variable in "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:371
msgid ""
"If specified twice, show in addition the unified diff between what would be "
"committed and the worktree files, i.e. the unstaged changes to tracked "
"files."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:372
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-q`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:373
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--quiet`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:375
msgid "Suppress commit summary message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:376
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--dry-run`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:380
msgid ""
"Do not create a commit, but show a list of paths that are to be committed, "
"paths with local changes that will be left uncommitted and paths that are "
"untracked."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:381
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--status`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:386
msgid ""
"Include the output of linkgit:git-status[1] in the commit message template "
"when using an editor to prepare the commit message.  Defaults to on, but can "
"be used to override configuration variable `commit.status`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:387
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-status`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:391
msgid ""
"Do not include the output of linkgit:git-status[1] in the commit message "
"template when using an editor to prepare the default commit message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:392
#, no-wrap
msgid "`-S[<key-id>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:393
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--gpg-sign[=<key-id>]`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:394
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--no-gpg-sign`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:400
msgid ""
"GPG-sign commits. The _<key-id>_ is optional and defaults to the committer "
"identity; if specified, it must be stuck to the option without a "
"space. `--no-gpg-sign` is useful to countermand both `commit.gpgSign` "
"configuration variable, and earlier `--gpg-sign`."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:401
#, no-wrap
msgid "`--`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:403
msgid "Do not interpret any more arguments as options."
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:404
#, no-wrap
msgid "`<pathspec>...`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:409
msgid ""
"When _<pathspec>_ is given on the command line, commit the contents of the "
"files that match the pathspec without recording the changes already added to "
"the index. The contents of these files are also staged for the next commit "
"on top of what have been staged before."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:411
msgid "For more details, see the 'pathspec' entry in linkgit:gitglossary[7]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:413
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:425
msgid ""
"When recording your own work, the contents of modified files in your working "
"tree are temporarily stored to a staging area called the \"index\" with `git "
"add`.  A file can be reverted back, only in the index but not in the working "
"tree, to that of the last commit with `git restore --staged <file>`, which "
"effectively reverts `git add` and prevents the changes to this file from "
"participating in the next commit.  After building the state to be committed "
"incrementally with these commands, `git commit` (without any pathname "
"parameter) is used to record what has been staged so far.  This is the most "
"basic form of the command.  An example:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:431
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ edit hello.c\n"
"$ git rm goodbye.c\n"
"$ git add hello.c\n"
"$ git commit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:439
msgid ""
"Instead of staging files after each individual change, you can tell `git "
"commit` to notice the changes to the files whose contents are tracked in "
"your working tree and do corresponding `git add` and `git rm` for you.  That "
"is, this example does the same as the earlier example if there is no other "
"change in your working tree:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:444
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ edit hello.c\n"
"$ rm goodbye.c\n"
"$ git commit -a\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:449
msgid ""
"The command `git commit -a` first looks at your working tree, notices that "
"you have modified `hello.c` and removed `goodbye.c`, and performs necessary "
"`git add` and `git rm` for you."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:454
msgid ""
"After staging changes to many files, you can alter the order the changes are "
"recorded in, by giving pathnames to `git commit`.  When pathnames are given, "
"the command makes a commit that only records the changes made to the named "
"paths:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:460
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ edit hello.c hello.h\n"
"$ git add hello.c hello.h\n"
"$ edit Makefile\n"
"$ git commit Makefile\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:467
msgid ""
"This makes a commit that records the modification to `Makefile`.  The "
"changes staged for `hello.c` and `hello.h` are not included in the resulting "
"commit.  However, their changes are not lost -- they are still staged and "
"merely held back.  After the above sequence, if you do:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:470
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:496
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git commit\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:474
msgid ""
"this second commit would record the changes to `hello.c` and `hello.h` as "
"expected."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:482
msgid ""
"After a merge (initiated by `git merge` or `git pull`) stops because of "
"conflicts, cleanly merged paths are already staged to be committed for you, "
"and paths that conflicted are left in unmerged state.  You would have to "
"first check which paths are conflicting with `git status` and after fixing "
"them manually in your working tree, you would stage the result as usual with "
"`git add`:"
msgstr ""

#. type: delimited block -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:488
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$ git status | grep unmerged\n"
"unmerged: hello.c\n"
"$ edit hello.c\n"
"$ git add hello.c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:493
msgid ""
"After resolving conflicts and staging the result, `git ls-files -u` would "
"stop mentioning the conflicted path.  When you are done, run `git commit` to "
"finally record the merge:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:504
msgid ""
"As with the case to record your own changes, you can use `-a` option to save "
"typing.  One difference is that during a merge resolution, you cannot use "
"`git commit` with pathnames to alter the order the changes are committed, "
"because the merge should be recorded as a single commit.  In fact, the "
"command refuses to run when given pathnames (but see `-i` option)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:506
#, no-wrap
msgid "COMMIT INFORMATION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:510
msgid ""
"Author and committer information is taken from the following environment "
"variables, if set:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:512
msgid "`GIT_AUTHOR_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:513
msgid "`GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:514
msgid "`GIT_AUTHOR_DATE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:515
msgid "`GIT_COMMITTER_NAME`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:516
msgid "`GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:517
msgid "`GIT_COMMITTER_DATE`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:519
msgid "(nb \"<\", \">\" and \"\\n\"s are stripped)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:525
msgid ""
"The author and committer names are by convention some form of a personal "
"name (that is, the name by which other humans refer to you), although Git "
"does not enforce or require any particular form. Arbitrary Unicode may be "
"used, subject to the constraints listed above. This name has no effect on "
"authentication; for that, see the `credential.username` variable in "
"linkgit:git-config[1]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:532
msgid ""
"In case (some of) these environment variables are not set, the information "
"is taken from the configuration items `user.name` and `user.email`, or, if "
"not present, the environment variable `EMAIL`, or, if that is not set, "
"system user name and the hostname used for outgoing mail (taken from "
"`/etc/mailname` and falling back to the fully qualified hostname when that "
"file does not exist)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:536
msgid ""
"The `author.name` and `committer.name` and their corresponding email options "
"override `user.name` and `user.email` if set and are overridden themselves "
"by the environment variables."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:539
msgid ""
"The typical usage is to set just the `user.name` and `user.email` variables; "
"the other options are provided for more complex use cases."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:544
#, no-wrap
msgid "DISCUSSION"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:553
msgid ""
"Though not required, it's a good idea to begin the commit message with a "
"single short (no more than 50 characters) line summarizing the change, "
"followed by a blank line and then a more thorough description.  The text up "
"to the first blank line in a commit message is treated as the commit title, "
"and that title is used throughout Git.  For example, "
"linkgit:git-format-patch[1] turns a commit into email, and it uses the title "
"on the Subject line and the rest of the commit in the body."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:557
#, no-wrap
msgid "ENVIRONMENT AND CONFIGURATION VARIABLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:562
msgid ""
"The editor used to edit the commit log message will be chosen from the "
"`GIT_EDITOR` environment variable, the `core.editor` configuration variable, "
"the `VISUAL` environment variable, or the `EDITOR` environment variable (in "
"that order).  See linkgit:git-var[1] for details."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:568
#, no-wrap
msgid "HOOKS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:572
msgid ""
"This command can run `commit-msg`, `prepare-commit-msg`, `pre-commit`, "
"`post-commit` and `post-rewrite` hooks.  See linkgit:githooks[5] for more "
"information."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title -
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:574
#, no-wrap
msgid "FILES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Labeled list
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:576
#, no-wrap
msgid "`$GIT_DIR/COMMIT_EDITMSG`"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: ../Documentation-sedout/git-commit.adoc:582
msgid ""
"This file contains the commit message of a commit in progress.  If `git "
"commit` exits due to an error before creating a commit, any commit message "
"that has been provided by the user (e.g., in an editor session) will be "
"available in this file, but will be overwritten by the next invocation of "
"`git commit`."
msgstr ""

